{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794146816,"nonce":"jvq+VGOIZVITwTNlq9SwPA+KjUE=","title":"iOS scroll momentum only advanced on frames that rebuilt the tree, so a flick stepped and stopped","message":"The iOS runner called `tick_scroll` (which runs `RenderTree::tick_scroll_physics`) only inside `build_ui`, and `needs_render` did not count a running fling as a reason to draw. A touch scroll moves content directly from the touch events, but after the finger lifts the `Decelerating` state advances only when a tick runs: here, on a rebuild. The tree rarely rebuilds while scrolling, so a flick moved in occasional steps and then stopped (\"pauses in between\", reported on an iPhone 12 Pro Max, 2026-11-08). Android ticks the physics every loop iteration and was not affected.\n\nFix: `blinc_build_frame` (and `blinc_tick_animations`) tick scroll physics every frame and record whether it is still animating; `needs_render` returns true while it is. Also in this change: iOS uses a velocity decay of 2 per second (ScrollConfig::deceleration 1000), the rate UIKit's normal deceleration uses, where 1500 coasted about two thirds as far; and a finger that rests more than 100 ms before lifting launches no fling. Bounce stays disabled on mobile; momentum is untouched on other platforms. Tests: widgets::scroll::tests in blinc_layout. The glide itself needs a hand on the device.","files":null}]}